Clone, compile and install EPA-ng (Barbera et al. 2019) into the phylopq
user data directory, where \code{\link[=is_epang_installed]{is_epang_installed()}} and \code{\link[=place_pq]{place_pq()}} look for
it. No change to your \code{PATH} is needed.

EPA-ng is a C++ project that carries its libraries as git submodules, so it
is fetched with a recursive \verb{git clone} rather than a release tarball, and
built with cmake. That needs \code{git}, \code{cmake}, \code{make}, a C++ compiler, and —
because the bundled \code{libpll} generates a parser — \code{bison} and \code{flex}. On
Debian/Ubuntu:
\verb{sudo apt install build-essential cmake git bison flex}. The build takes
several minutes.

\strong{\verb{conda install -c bioconda epa-ng} is quicker} whenever conda is
available; this function exists for machines where it is not.
